        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$(\\log_3 x)^2=\\log_3(x^2)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=1,9$.",
          "worked": "The original domain is $x>0$. Hence $\\log_3(x^2)=2\\log_3 x$. Let $u=\\log_3 x$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}u^2&=2u\\\\u(u-2)&=0\\\\u&=0\\text{ or }2\\\\x&=1\\text{ or }9\\end{align*}$$\n\nBoth values satisfy the original domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-stem-47-49-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\ln\\sqrt{2x+1}-\\ln\\sqrt{3x+1}=\\ln 3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=-\\dfrac{8}{25}$.",
          "worked": "The logarithm arguments must be positive, so $2x+1>0$ and $3x+1>0$. Together these require $x>-\\frac13$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\ln\\sqrt{\\frac{2x+1}{3x+1}}&=\\ln 3\\\\\\sqrt{\\frac{2x+1}{3x+1}}&=3\\\\\\frac{2x+1}{3x+1}&=9\\\\2x+1&=27x+9\\\\25x&=-8\\\\x&=-\\frac{8}{25}\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{8}{25}>-\\frac13$, this is a valid solution.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 82
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-stem-47-49-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$(\\ln(x^2))^2-6\\ln(x^2)+8=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
